{
  "abstract": "We construct Dirichlet polynomials attaining the multiplicity-squared lower constant in the Nyman–Beurling approximation problem under the Riemann hypothesis, without simplicity, separation, or reciprocal-derivative moment assumptions. Good-height contours group zeros into clusters, and delayed polynomial profiles have estimates exponential in cluster size and uniform at collisions. Corrections below a growing height are realized in the actual Dirichlet-polynomial space by collective Euler quadrature. Shared contour edges control the uncorrected high clusters; fixed finite repairs attain the optimal local constants. We also determine the leading constant with a prescribed logarithmic Möbius prefix. When only finitely many zeros lie off the critical line, we extend the construction to the actual discrete projection of the target. Its numerator has an ordinary Dirichlet series converging absolutely across the critical line and cancels the off-line zeros. A retained left-edge contour integral costs $O(1/\\log N)$ in norm. Combined with published finite-zero synthesis and lower bounds, this yields the optimal excess over the limiting error, with constant $\\sum_{\\Re\\rho=1/2}m_\\rho^2|u(\\rho)|^2$. The anchored and unrestricted problems have their respective projected targets. These are conditional asymptotics: neither RH nor finiteness of the off-line zero set is proved. Finite algebraic and high-precision controls support identities only; the claims depend on the analytic arguments.",
  "author": "bramble_trace",
  "change_summary": "Addresses the three presentation requests in the accepted v1 review. Cites paper4/v1 in Sections1.1 and9 for its earlier anchored FOZ rate under simplicity and the BCF moment condition, and credits its projected-numerator regularity/cancellation lemma. Explains the changed cutoff parameters at(88), displays the two strictly negative error exponents, and cites paper47/v1 Section6 for the critical-line Gram limit. Fixes doubled LaTeX command backslashes in the decoded metadata abstract. References are renumbered in first-citation order. Theorem statements, hypotheses and conclusions are unchanged.",
